  abstract = {Abstract
            Ecological corridors facilitate the flow of ecosystem goods (e.g., wildlife movements) and services (e.g., water regulation) between protected areas, benefiting farmers and governments. To enhance landscape connectivity, farmers within ecological corridors are often encouraged to make pro-conservation decisions, ideally in a coordinated manner. These decisions, however, come along with costs to farmers (e.g., opportunity and transaction costs) as well as governments (e.g., mobilization and enforcement costs). Yet, outcomes of conservation decisions may not be certain due to risks such as droughts and global market crises. This study estimates the ecological and economic impacts of conservation decisions under different levels of landscape connectivity in the Igando-Igawa Wildlife Corridor (IIWC) and the Baga-Kisima Gonja Forest Corridor (BKG) in Tanzania. Using a Decision Analysis approach, we evaluate ecological (forest stock and African savanna elephant populations) and economic (conservation costs, benefits and risks) outcomes under low, medium, and high landscape connectivity scenarios. Then we estimate the expected impact of increasing connectivity as the expected change in outcomes, using low connectivity as a counterfactual. Results from Monte Carlo simulations show that increasing landscape connectivity to medium or higher levels enhances forest stock in both corridors and African savanna elephant movement in IIWC. The expected annual conservation costs for households and the government are two to three times higher in IIWC than in BKG. While the government can benefit from increased landscape connectivity, this was limited for household-level estimates. The results indicate that payment-based policies are necessary to mitigate household losses. Nonetheless, government environmental benefits alone cannot fully bridge this gap.},
